Title
The Last Turn of Sethan Valley
Act 1: The Joke
Characters
Dev (20) – playful, impulsive, younger brother.
Dhruv (25) – confident driver, practical but enjoys pranks.
Dua (25) – brave on the surface, secretly uneasy about isolated places.
Opening
The three siblings are driving through Sethan Valley at night in their Kia Sonet.
The roads are narrow, surrounded by dark pine forests and steep drops.
Mist slowly begins covering the road.
Dua keeps glancing outside but acts fearless.
First Prank
Dev and Dhruv pretend the engine has failed.
Dhruv intentionally lets the car roll backward on a slope.
Dev acts panicked:
"Bhai, brakes bhi nahi lag rahe!"
Dua immediately realizes they're joking.
She calmly checks her phone and ignores them.
The brothers are disappointed that she didn't get scared.
Act 2: The Traveller
Strange Encounter
Around a blind turn, they see a white traveller van stopped on the roadside.
About twenty passengers are sitting inside.
The driver and 4–5 passengers are standing outside discussing something nervously.
The Conversation
Dhruv lowers the window.
"Kya hua bhaiya?"
The driver replies:
"Villa bas 3-4 kilometer aage hai... lekin koi jaana nahi chahta."
What They Learn
The group booked an isolated villa in the mountains.
While approaching, they repeatedly saw:
A woman standing beside the road.
The same woman appearing ahead again after every turn.
Some passengers claim she was getting closer each time.
One elderly passenger refuses to continue.
Another passenger says:
"Uske pair ulte the."
Reaction
Dev laughs and assumes they're drunk or imagining things.
Dhruv thanks them and drives away.
Dua notices none of the passengers are laughing.
Act 3: The Woman on the Road
First Sighting
A few minutes later, they see a lone woman standing beside the road.
Long black hair.
White top normal black pants.
Rational Explanations
Dev says:
"Dekha? Yahi hogi unki bhootni." Dua says: "Piche mat dekhna samne dekho" and starts praying
Dhruv drives past.
Something Wrong
After several turns they see her again and she is on legs still coming before them.
Same posture.
Same clothes.
Same location relative to the road.
Growing Fear
Dua notices:
"Usne sir uthaya tha."
The brothers insist she's imagining things.The Last Turn of Sethan Valley (Continued & Completed)
Act 3: The Woman on the Road (Continued)
Dhruv tightened his grip on the steering wheel.
“Arre, it’s the same woman those people were talking about. Relax. She’s probably just a local walking home.”
Dev turned around in the passenger seat, grinning. “Bhai, imagine if her feet really are backwards. We should stop and ask for a selfie.”
“Shut up, Dev,” Dua snapped, her voice sharper than she intended. She kept her eyes fixed forward, lips moving silently in prayer. The car’s headlights cut through the thickening mist like a knife through milk.
They took three more blind turns.
She was there again.
Same white top. Same long black hair covering half her face. Standing exactly where the previous sighting had been — as if the road itself was looping her back in front of them.
This time Dhruv didn’t joke. He slowed down but didn’t stop. As they passed, Dua stole a glance.
The woman slowly lifted her head.
For one terrifying second, her eyes met Dua’s. They were completely white — no pupils, just milky voids. A small smile crept across her lips.
Dua’s stomach dropped. “Drive faster,” she whispered.
“What?”
“Drive faster, Dhruv!”
The engine roared as Dhruv pressed the accelerator. The Kia Sonet shot forward. Dev, who had finally stopped laughing, kept looking back.
“Bhai… she’s still standing there. But… her head is turned. She’s watching us.”
Act 4: The Loop
The GPS on Dua’s phone started glitching. The blue arrow showed them moving, but the map kept resetting to the same stretch of road.
“Villa is 800 meters ahead,” Dhruv muttered, trying to sound calm.
They crossed the 800-meter mark.
The woman appeared again.
This time she was closer to the road. Almost on the white line.
Dev’s voice cracked. “This isn’t funny anymore.”
Dhruv swerved slightly to create distance. As they passed, they heard it — a soft, wet sound, like bare feet on cold tar, even though the woman hadn’t moved.
Dua started breathing heavily. “Her feet… Dev, check her feet.”
Dev pressed his face against the glass. “I can’t see properly… the hair is in the way… wait—”
Suddenly the woman turned her entire body toward them — unnaturally fast, like her neck and torso weren’t connected properly.
Her feet were facing the opposite direction.
Dev screamed.
Dhruv floored the accelerator. The car flew through the mist. Trees blurred past. The road began climbing again.
For three full minutes, there was nothing behind them.
Then Dua whispered, “She’s in the rearview mirror.”
Dhruv looked up. The woman was standing in the middle of the road behind them, now much closer than physics should allow. Her head was tilted at an impossible angle.
Act 5: The Last Turn
The GPS finally said: “You have reached your destination.”
A beautiful wooden villa appeared on the left, warm lights glowing from its windows. Relief flooded the siblings.
Dhruv turned the car toward the gate.
As the car slowed, Dev looked out his window and froze.
The woman was standing right beside his door.
